  "explanation": "Let $\\sin^{-1}\\frac{3}{4} = \\theta$. Then $\\sin\\theta = \\frac{3}{4}$.<br /><br />Thus $\\cos\\theta = \\sqrt{1-\\frac{9}{16}} = \\frac{\\sqrt7}{4}$.<br /><br />Let $\\alpha = \\frac{\\theta}{2}$. Then $\\theta = 2\\alpha$.<br /><br />Using identity $\\sin2\\alpha = 2\\sin\\alpha\\cos\\alpha = \\frac{3}{4}$.<br /><br />Also $\\cos2\\alpha = 2\\cos^2\\alpha - 1 = \\frac{\\sqrt7}{4}$.<br /><br />From this $\\cos^2\\alpha = \\frac{4+\\sqrt7}{8}$.<br /><br />Also $\\sin\\alpha\\cos\\alpha = \\frac{3}{8}$.<br /><br />Thus $\\tan\\alpha = \\frac{\\sin\\alpha}{\\cos\\alpha} = \\frac{3/8}{(4+\\sqrt7)/8} = \\frac{3}{4+\\sqrt7}$.<br /><br />Rationalising:<br /><br />$\\tan\\alpha = \\frac{3(4-\\sqrt7)}{16-7} = \\frac{4-\\sqrt7}{3}$.<br /><br />Hence $\\alpha = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{4-\\sqrt7}{3}\\right)$.",
